 "policy_prompt_for_subgoal": "The failure was at the first decision step of a push demonstration in which the gripper happened to come down beside the stick: the fingers showed a roughly 19% reading for one frame while closing, then 0% at table height, and the cube slid to the target while the stick was nudged aside. The policy wrote `hook` and chose A (pick up the peg) where the recorded subgoal was C (push). This breaks PREDICTABILITY of `method` (the instruction let a closing transient count as a hook grasp) and so SUFFICIENCY of the first subgoal. The subgoal rules themselves need two changes. First, the rule that chooses by `method` at the first decision step must say that `method` is decided from the transport frames and the maximum raised height, as the memory instruction now states, so that a push demonstration that brushed the stick still yields C. Second, the later-step rules currently tell the policy to judge the fingers and rise alone and treat the scene as sufficient, which is why `method` was carried and never read; replace them with: at every later decision step read `method` first and use the scene only to locate the position in that method's chain. If `method` is `hook`: fingers at about 20-25% and gripper risen after a descent onto the stick -> B; otherwise (fingers fully open or at 0%, nothing raised) -> A. If `method` is `pick`: fingers at about 40-60% and risen after a descent onto the cube -> E; otherwise -> D. If `method` is `push`: C in every situation. Keep the guard that B and E are never chosen without the matching finger opening and rise. Drop the sentence that says the marking of an object as held is unreliable and should be ignored; the marking is one of the cues that separates hook from push. The `method` field is kept: it is the only record of the demonstration and is what decides between A, D and C whenever a grasp closes on nothing or reopens, and under the rewritten rules it is read at every later step rather than only inferred from the scene."}
